1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,904
He did not come from racing.

2
00:00:02,084 --> 00:00:03,986
He did not come from a famous

3
00:00:03,986 --> 00:00:04,707
car family.

4
00:00:04,887 --> 00:00:06,660
He came from a farm town in

5
00:00:06,660 --> 00:00:09,550
northern Italy, he repaired military trucks,

6
00:00:09,550 --> 00:00:10,994
and he built tractors.

7
00:00:11,174 --> 00:00:13,559
Then, in nineteen sixty-three,

8
00:00:13,559 --> 00:00:15,547
he started a car company,

9
00:00:15,547 --> 00:00:19,125
and eleven years later his engineers launched

10
00:00:19,125 --> 00:00:21,112
the Lamborghini Countach.

11
00:00:21,292 --> 00:00:23,472
This is how a tractor maker ended

12
00:00:23,472 --> 00:00:25,718
up building one of the most famous

13
00:00:25,718 --> 00:00:27,039
supercars ever made.

14
00:00:27,390 --> 00:00:30,126
The usual version of this story has

15
00:00:30,126 --> 00:00:33,253
a single angry moment with Enzo Ferrari.

16
00:00:33,433 --> 00:00:34,702
The real record,

17
00:00:34,702 --> 00:00:36,686
according to our sources,

18
00:00:36,686 --> 00:00:38,273
is more complicated,

19
00:00:38,273 --> 00:00:40,178
and in places uncertain.

20
00:00:40,358 --> 00:00:42,045
We will tell it in order,

21
00:00:42,045 --> 00:00:43,597
say what is documented,

22
00:00:43,597 --> 00:00:45,757
and mark clearly what is legend.

23
00:00:45,937 --> 00:00:48,778
Every fact comes from a published source,

24
00:00:48,778 --> 00:00:50,581
listed in the description.

25
00:00:50,761 --> 00:00:52,429
Where we do the maths,

26
00:00:52,429 --> 00:00:53,187
we say so.

27
00:00:53,537 --> 00:00:57,353
Wikipedia says Ferruccio Lamborghini was born on

28
00:00:57,353 --> 00:00:59,499
the twenty-eighth of April,

29
00:00:59,499 --> 00:01:02,520
nineteen sixteen, in Renazzo di Cento,

30
00:01:02,520 --> 00:01:05,541
in the Emilia-Romagna region of Italy.

31
00:01:05,721 --> 00:01:08,734
His parents, Antonio and Evelina,

32
00:01:08,734 --> 00:01:10,377
were wine growers.

33
00:01:10,557 --> 00:01:12,660
The page says he was drawn more

34
00:01:12,660 --> 00:01:14,967
to farm machinery than to farming,

35
00:01:14,967 --> 00:01:17,274
and that he studied at a technical

36
00:01:17,274 --> 00:01:18,835
institute near Bologna.

37
00:01:19,184 --> 00:01:21,193
What he studied is disputed.

38
00:01:21,373 --> 00:01:24,207
Wikipedia says the sources disagree:

39
00:01:24,207 --> 00:01:27,198
one says engineering without a degree,

40
00:01:27,198 --> 00:01:29,245
another industrial design,

41
00:01:29,245 --> 00:01:31,921
a third an industrial arts degree,

42
00:01:31,921 --> 00:01:34,440
and a fourth says he was drafted

43
00:01:34,440 --> 00:01:37,116
upon graduating in nineteen forty.

44
00:01:37,296 --> 00:01:39,200
We do not choose between them.

45
00:01:39,380 --> 00:01:41,524
The point is that even the basic

46
00:01:41,524 --> 00:01:44,136
facts of his education are not settled,

47
00:01:44,136 --> 00:01:46,681
which is a warning for everything that

48
00:01:46,681 --> 00:01:47,217
follows.

49
00:01:47,566 --> 00:01:50,283
In nineteen forty he was drafted into

50
00:01:50,283 --> 00:01:52,338
the Italian Royal Air Force.

51
00:01:52,518 --> 00:01:54,884
He served as a mechanic on the

52
00:01:54,884 --> 00:01:56,225
island of Rhodes,

53
00:01:56,225 --> 00:02:00,011
and became supervisor of the vehicle maintenance

54
00:02:00,011 --> 00:02:01,667
unit, Wikipedia says.

55
00:02:01,847 --> 00:02:05,412
After Italy's armistice in nineteen forty-three he

56
00:02:05,412 --> 00:02:06,910
stayed on the island,

57
00:02:06,910 --> 00:02:09,976
opened a small vehicle workshop with German

58
00:02:09,976 --> 00:02:13,257
permission, and was arrested as a collaborator

59
00:02:13,257 --> 00:02:16,109
when the British took Rhodes in nineteen

60
00:02:16,109 --> 00:02:16,893
forty-five.

61
00:02:17,243 --> 00:02:20,413
He returned to Italy in nineteen forty-six.

62
00:02:20,593 --> 00:02:21,930
By our arithmetic,

63
00:02:21,930 --> 00:02:23,712
he was thirty years old,

64
00:02:23,712 --> 00:02:26,756
with six years of military service behind

65
00:02:26,756 --> 00:02:29,132
him and a skill that happened to

66
00:02:29,132 --> 00:02:30,098
be in demand:

67
00:02:30,098 --> 00:02:33,736
keeping machines running with whatever parts were

68
00:02:33,736 --> 00:02:34,479
available.

69
00:02:34,659 --> 00:02:36,597
That is our reading of the timeline.

70
00:02:36,777 --> 00:02:39,076
The sources say only that he went

71
00:02:39,076 --> 00:02:41,514
back and began to work with surplus

72
00:02:41,514 --> 00:02:42,141
vehicles.

73
00:02:42,491 --> 00:02:46,162
Wikipedia says he bought surplus military vehicles

74
00:02:46,162 --> 00:02:49,539
and converted them into agricultural machines.

75
00:02:49,719 --> 00:02:51,116
His first tractors,

76
00:02:51,116 --> 00:02:52,513
called the Carioca,

77
00:02:52,513 --> 00:02:54,572
around nineteen forty-seven,

78
00:02:54,572 --> 00:02:57,292
used engines from Morris trucks and a

79
00:02:57,292 --> 00:02:59,939
fuel atomiser that let them start on

80
00:02:59,939 --> 00:03:02,734
petrol and then run on cheaper diesel.

81
00:03:02,914 --> 00:03:05,870
The page gives nineteen forty-eight as the

82
00:03:05,870 --> 00:03:08,475
year he founded Lamborghini Trattori.

83
00:03:08,824 --> 00:03:11,499
The tractors made him rich enough to

84
00:03:11,499 --> 00:03:12,540
buy fast cars.

85
00:03:12,720 --> 00:03:15,170
Wikipedia lists Alfa Romeos,

86
00:03:15,170 --> 00:03:18,934
Lancias, a Mercedes-Benz three hundred S L,

87
00:03:18,934 --> 00:03:22,785
a Jaguar E-Type and two Maserati thirty-five

88
00:03:22,785 --> 00:03:23,923
hundred G Ts.

89
00:03:24,103 --> 00:03:27,068
It also says he bought several Ferrari

90
00:03:27,068 --> 00:03:28,239
two fifty G Ts,

91
00:03:28,239 --> 00:03:31,594
among them a short wheelbase Berlinetta and

92
00:03:31,594 --> 00:03:32,764
a two plus two.

93
00:03:32,944 --> 00:03:34,633
So he was a customer of the

94
00:03:34,633 --> 00:03:36,822
people he would later compete with.

95
00:03:37,172 --> 00:03:39,297
He built other businesses too.

96
00:03:39,477 --> 00:03:41,328
In nineteen fifty-nine,

97
00:03:41,328 --> 00:03:42,454
the page says,

98
00:03:42,454 --> 00:03:45,029
he opened an oil burner factory,

99
00:03:45,029 --> 00:03:49,454
Lamborghini Bruciatori, later called Lamborghini Calor,

100
00:03:49,454 --> 00:03:52,190
which moved into air conditioning.

101
00:03:52,370 --> 00:03:57,182
In nineteen sixty-nine he founded Lamborghini Oleodinamica,

102
00:03:57,182 --> 00:03:58,976
a hydraulics business.

103
00:03:59,156 --> 00:04:00,589
By our arithmetic,

104
00:04:00,589 --> 00:04:02,499
he was running tractors,

105
00:04:02,499 --> 00:04:06,081
heating and hydraulics before the car company

106
00:04:06,081 --> 00:04:07,991
was even four years old.

107
00:04:08,341 --> 00:04:09,769
Then came the cars.

108
00:04:09,949 --> 00:04:14,948
Automobili Lamborghini was founded in nineteen sixty-three

109
00:04:14,948 --> 00:04:17,016
in Sant'Agata Bolognese,

110
00:04:17,016 --> 00:04:18,309
Wikipedia says.

111
00:04:18,489 --> 00:04:19,889
By our arithmetic,

112
00:04:19,889 --> 00:04:21,368
he was forty-seven.

113
00:04:21,548 --> 00:04:23,938
The page says he aimed to build

114
00:04:23,938 --> 00:04:26,790
a grand tourer with high performance,

115
00:04:26,790 --> 00:04:30,337
tractability, ride quality and good interiors.

116
00:04:30,517 --> 00:04:31,810
Note the words:

117
00:04:31,810 --> 00:04:33,104
not a race car,

118
00:04:33,104 --> 00:04:34,743
a refined road car.

119
00:04:34,923 --> 00:04:37,047
That matters for what happened next.

120
00:04:37,397 --> 00:04:39,726
The page notes that the first two

121
00:04:39,726 --> 00:04:41,983
cars carried alphanumeric names.

122
00:04:42,163 --> 00:04:44,149
The bull names started with the Miura.

123
00:04:44,329 --> 00:04:46,927
Wikipedia says he visited the ranch of

124
00:04:46,927 --> 00:04:49,593
the Miura family of fighting bulls near

125
00:04:49,593 --> 00:04:51,644
Seville in nineteen sixty-two,

126
00:04:51,644 --> 00:04:53,969
and the Miura was the fourth model

127
00:04:53,969 --> 00:04:54,789
he produced.

128
00:04:54,969 --> 00:04:57,750
Other bull names followed:

129
00:04:57,750 --> 00:05:00,209
Islero, Espada, Jarama,

130
00:05:00,209 --> 00:05:02,562
Urraco, Jalpa, Diablo.

131
00:05:02,742 --> 00:05:04,809
The Countach broke the pattern.

132
00:05:05,158 --> 00:05:06,285
Now the legend.

133
00:05:06,465 --> 00:05:10,892
Wikipedia says Ferruccio Lamborghini complained that Ferrari's

134
00:05:10,892 --> 00:05:14,534
clutches were inferior and needed repeated rebuilds

135
00:05:14,534 --> 00:05:15,462
at Maranello.

136
00:05:15,642 --> 00:05:18,126
He raised the issue with Enzo Ferrari

137
00:05:18,126 --> 00:05:19,334
and was dismissed.

138
00:05:19,514 --> 00:05:23,059
In a nineteen ninety-one interview with Thoroughbred

139
00:05:23,059 --> 00:05:24,218
and Classic Cars,

140
00:05:24,218 --> 00:05:25,172
the page says,

141
00:05:25,172 --> 00:05:27,899
he recalled Ferrari telling him he could

142
00:05:27,899 --> 00:05:30,216
drive a tractor but never handle a

143
00:05:30,216 --> 00:05:32,466
Ferrari, which led him to build a

144
00:05:32,466 --> 00:05:33,284
perfect car.

145
00:05:33,633 --> 00:05:34,591
He also said,

146
00:05:34,591 --> 00:05:36,211
according to the page,

147
00:05:36,211 --> 00:05:39,009
that Ferrari never spoke to him again.

148
00:05:39,189 --> 00:05:41,882
That is the whole of the documented

149
00:05:41,882 --> 00:05:44,498
story: a complaint about clutches,

150
00:05:44,498 --> 00:05:47,806
a dismissal, a remark recalled thirty years

151
00:05:47,806 --> 00:05:48,268
later.

152
00:05:48,448 --> 00:05:51,065
Wikipedia itself says the tale is as

153
00:05:51,065 --> 00:05:53,246
much the stuff of legend as of

154
00:05:53,246 --> 00:05:56,081
fact, and that his motives for entering

155
00:05:56,081 --> 00:05:58,189
the car business are unclear.

156
00:05:58,538 --> 00:06:00,951
Wikipedia also cites an author,

157
00:06:00,951 --> 00:06:04,453
Sackey, who argues that economic motives were

158
00:06:04,453 --> 00:06:06,398
stronger than the insult.

159
00:06:06,578 --> 00:06:08,804
That is the sceptic's version:

160
00:06:08,804 --> 00:06:12,365
a businessman who already owned several Ferraris

161
00:06:12,365 --> 00:06:13,626
and saw a market.

162
00:06:13,806 --> 00:06:15,821
We do not know which version is

163
00:06:15,821 --> 00:06:18,032
right, and our sources do not say.

164
00:06:18,212 --> 00:06:20,622
The honest summary is that the tractor

165
00:06:20,622 --> 00:06:22,461
line is a good story that may

166
00:06:22,461 --> 00:06:24,110
be only part of the truth.

167
00:06:24,459 --> 00:06:26,349
There is a lesson in how the

168
00:06:26,349 --> 00:06:27,699
story has been used.

169
00:06:27,879 --> 00:06:30,386
A tractor maker beating Ferrari is a

170
00:06:30,386 --> 00:06:33,591
more satisfying tale than a rich industrialist

171
00:06:33,591 --> 00:06:34,636
spotting a gap.

172
00:06:34,816 --> 00:06:37,590
But the evidence we have shows both:

173
00:06:37,590 --> 00:06:38,900
he made tractors,

174
00:06:38,900 --> 00:06:40,364
he bought Ferraris,

175
00:06:40,364 --> 00:06:42,829
he built a refined grand tourer,

176
00:06:42,829 --> 00:06:45,218
and he later left the business.

177
00:06:45,398 --> 00:06:48,173
Whether pride or profit started it,

178
00:06:48,173 --> 00:06:50,472
the result was a car company.

179
00:06:50,821 --> 00:06:52,230
The Miura came next,

180
00:06:52,230 --> 00:06:53,921
and it was not his idea.

181
00:06:54,101 --> 00:06:57,707
Wikipedia says the engineers Giampaolo Dallara,

182
00:06:57,707 --> 00:07:01,083
Paolo Stanzani and Bob Wallace developed the

183
00:07:01,083 --> 00:07:03,385
prototype in their spare time,

184
00:07:03,385 --> 00:07:04,690
largely at night.

185
00:07:04,870 --> 00:07:09,088
Ferruccio preferred grand tourers to race-derived cars,

186
00:07:09,088 --> 00:07:12,157
but after seeing the development mule he

187
00:07:12,157 --> 00:07:14,841
approved and gave them a free hand,

188
00:07:14,841 --> 00:07:17,756
seeing the car as a possible marketing

189
00:07:17,756 --> 00:07:18,140
tool.

190
00:07:18,489 --> 00:07:21,488
So the car that made Lamborghini famous

191
00:07:21,488 --> 00:07:24,640
started as an unofficial night project by

192
00:07:24,640 --> 00:07:26,024
his own engineers.

193
00:07:26,204 --> 00:07:29,005
The rolling chassis appeared at the Turin

194
00:07:29,005 --> 00:07:30,917
show in nineteen sixty-five,

195
00:07:30,917 --> 00:07:33,923
and showgoers placed orders before there was

196
00:07:33,923 --> 00:07:34,401
a body.

197
00:07:34,581 --> 00:07:37,889
The Bertone body was finished days before

198
00:07:37,889 --> 00:07:40,955
the Geneva show of nineteen sixty-six.

199
00:07:41,135 --> 00:07:42,428
By our arithmetic,

200
00:07:42,428 --> 00:07:44,944
from prototype to show took about a

201
00:07:44,944 --> 00:07:45,303
year.

202
00:07:45,652 --> 00:07:47,665
Wikipedia describes the show:

203
00:07:47,665 --> 00:07:50,511
the engineers had not checked whether the

204
00:07:50,511 --> 00:07:52,038
engine fitted the bay.

205
00:07:52,218 --> 00:07:54,555
They filled it with ballast and kept

206
00:07:54,555 --> 00:07:55,724
the bonnet locked,

207
00:07:55,724 --> 00:07:58,321
and sales head Ubaldo Sgarzi turned away

208
00:07:58,321 --> 00:08:00,333
journalists who wanted to look.

209
00:08:00,513 --> 00:08:03,044
The styling of Marcello Gandini and the

210
00:08:03,044 --> 00:08:05,444
mid-engine layout made it the star of

211
00:08:05,444 --> 00:08:06,028
the show.

212
00:08:06,208 --> 00:08:08,380
Whatever Ferruccio had planned,

213
00:08:08,380 --> 00:08:10,202
the Miura gave him a name.

214
00:08:10,551 --> 00:08:12,066
The Miura's engine,

215
00:08:12,066 --> 00:08:15,017
Wikipedia says, was a three thousand,

216
00:08:15,017 --> 00:08:19,083
nine hundred twenty-nine cubic centimetre V twelve,

217
00:08:19,083 --> 00:08:21,874
mounted sideways behind the driver,

218
00:08:21,874 --> 00:08:24,983
with the engine and gearbox sharing one

219
00:08:24,983 --> 00:08:25,621
casting.

220
00:08:25,801 --> 00:08:29,193
The page calls it the first high-performance

221
00:08:29,193 --> 00:08:32,508
production road car with a rear mid-engine,

222
00:08:32,508 --> 00:08:34,358
rear-wheel-drive layout.

223
00:08:34,538 --> 00:08:37,590
The V twelve itself dates from nineteen

224
00:08:37,590 --> 00:08:41,504
sixty-three and was designed by Giotto Bizzarrini.

225
00:08:41,853 --> 00:08:43,686
Just as the cars took off,

226
00:08:43,686 --> 00:08:45,801
the tractors ran into trouble.

227
00:08:45,981 --> 00:08:50,250
Wikipedia says Lamborghini Trattori struggled in nineteen

228
00:08:50,250 --> 00:08:54,070
seventy-one and seventy-two after its South African

229
00:08:54,070 --> 00:08:57,740
importer cancelled orders and a Bolivian military

230
00:08:57,740 --> 00:09:00,586
government cancelled a large shipment.

231
00:09:00,766 --> 00:09:03,183
Those are the two causes the page

232
00:09:03,183 --> 00:09:03,622
names.

233
00:09:03,802 --> 00:09:05,713
It does not give the sizes of

234
00:09:05,713 --> 00:09:08,085
the orders, so we cannot put numbers

235
00:09:08,085 --> 00:09:08,876
on the loss.

236
00:09:09,225 --> 00:09:11,071
In nineteen seventy-two,

237
00:09:11,071 --> 00:09:12,148
the page says,

238
00:09:12,148 --> 00:09:14,994
he sold his entire tractor holding to

239
00:09:14,994 --> 00:09:15,379
SAME.

240
00:09:15,559 --> 00:09:19,001
Many retellings give nineteen seventy-three for that

241
00:09:19,001 --> 00:09:19,332
sale.

242
00:09:19,512 --> 00:09:22,586
The Wikipedia page we used says nineteen

243
00:09:22,586 --> 00:09:24,738
seventy-two, so we use that,

244
00:09:24,738 --> 00:09:26,582
and note the difference.

245
00:09:26,762 --> 00:09:29,633
The company that gave him his fortune,

246
00:09:29,633 --> 00:09:30,615
and his name,

247
00:09:30,615 --> 00:09:31,975
was no longer his.

248
00:09:32,325 --> 00:09:34,474
Also in nineteen seventy-two,

249
00:09:34,474 --> 00:09:38,252
he sold fifty-one percent of Automobili Lamborghini

250
00:09:38,252 --> 00:09:42,031
to the Swiss businessman Georges-Henri Rossetti for

251
00:09:42,031 --> 00:09:44,179
six hundred thousand dollars.

252
00:09:44,359 --> 00:09:45,645
By our arithmetic,

253
00:09:45,645 --> 00:09:48,358
that values the whole company at about

254
00:09:48,358 --> 00:09:50,501
one point two million dollars.

255
00:09:50,681 --> 00:09:53,171
The car business that would later produce

256
00:09:53,171 --> 00:09:54,204
the Countach was,

257
00:09:54,204 --> 00:09:56,086
for him, a fraction of what the

258
00:09:56,086 --> 00:09:58,030
tractor business had been worth.

259
00:09:58,379 --> 00:10:00,270
In nineteen seventy-four,

260
00:10:00,270 --> 00:10:02,540
Wikipedia says, disillusioned,

261
00:10:02,540 --> 00:10:05,792
he sold his remaining forty-nine percent to

262
00:10:05,792 --> 00:10:08,364
René Leimer, a friend of Rossetti.

263
00:10:08,544 --> 00:10:10,613
The page gives no price for that

264
00:10:10,613 --> 00:10:11,388
second sale.

265
00:10:11,568 --> 00:10:12,822
By our arithmetic,

266
00:10:12,822 --> 00:10:15,051
he had owned the car company for

267
00:10:15,051 --> 00:10:18,604
eleven years, from nineteen sixty-three to nineteen

268
00:10:18,604 --> 00:10:21,878
seventy-four, and the tractor company for about

269
00:10:21,878 --> 00:10:26,267
twenty-four, from nineteen forty-eight to nineteen seventy-two.

270
00:10:26,616 --> 00:10:29,728
Meanwhile, the next car was being built.

271
00:10:29,908 --> 00:10:31,518
Wikipedia says that,

272
00:10:31,518 --> 00:10:33,127
by nineteen seventy,

273
00:10:33,127 --> 00:10:35,944
the Miura had lost ground to rivals

274
00:10:35,944 --> 00:10:38,197
such as the Ferrari Daytona.

275
00:10:38,377 --> 00:10:41,996
Ferruccio Lamborghini launched a project to replace

276
00:10:41,996 --> 00:10:42,209
it.

277
00:10:42,389 --> 00:10:45,873
Chief engineer Paolo Stanzani started work in

278
00:10:45,873 --> 00:10:48,582
nineteen seventy under the name L P

279
00:10:48,582 --> 00:10:51,757
one twelve, with test driver Bob Wallace,

280
00:10:51,757 --> 00:10:56,015
assistant engineer Massimo Parenti and Marcello Gandini

281
00:10:56,015 --> 00:10:56,866
of Bertone.

282
00:10:57,216 --> 00:11:00,042
The prototype, the L P five hundred,

283
00:11:00,042 --> 00:11:02,476
was shown at the Geneva show in

284
00:11:02,476 --> 00:11:04,124
nineteen seventy-one.

285
00:11:04,304 --> 00:11:07,047
The production L P four hundred debuted

286
00:11:07,047 --> 00:11:08,805
in nineteen seventy-four,

287
00:11:08,805 --> 00:11:10,493
about three years later.

288
00:11:10,673 --> 00:11:12,468
That is the same year the page

289
00:11:12,468 --> 00:11:14,504
says he sold his remaining shares.

290
00:11:14,684 --> 00:11:16,380
By our reading of the dates,

291
00:11:16,380 --> 00:11:19,046
the car he commissioned reached customers as

292
00:11:19,046 --> 00:11:19,955
he walked away.

293
00:11:20,304 --> 00:11:22,366
The sources do not tell us how

294
00:11:22,366 --> 00:11:25,183
involved he was in the Countach's design,

295
00:11:25,183 --> 00:11:27,107
so we cannot say how much of

296
00:11:27,107 --> 00:11:27,863
it was his.

297
00:11:28,043 --> 00:11:29,958
They say he launched the project.

298
00:11:30,138 --> 00:11:32,654
They say the engineers and the stylist

299
00:11:32,654 --> 00:11:33,250
built it.

300
00:11:33,430 --> 00:11:35,095
They do not quote him on the

301
00:11:35,095 --> 00:11:35,868
finished car.

302
00:11:36,048 --> 00:11:38,407
We avoid attributing views to him that

303
00:11:38,407 --> 00:11:40,146
the record does not contain.

304
00:11:40,496 --> 00:11:42,389
The name was another departure.

305
00:11:42,569 --> 00:11:45,947
Wikipedia says it comes from a Piedmontese

306
00:11:45,947 --> 00:11:48,200
exclamation of astonishment.

307
00:11:48,380 --> 00:11:51,323
Marcello Gandini says the word was first

308
00:11:51,323 --> 00:11:53,237
used as a joke by a worker

309
00:11:53,237 --> 00:11:54,488
and then adopted,

310
00:11:54,488 --> 00:11:56,769
and he calls this the only true

311
00:11:56,769 --> 00:11:57,946
story behind it.

312
00:11:58,126 --> 00:12:01,305
The popular tale of Nuccio Bertone exclaiming

313
00:12:01,305 --> 00:12:03,777
the word on seeing the prototype is

314
00:12:03,777 --> 00:12:05,966
marked on the page as needing a

315
00:12:05,966 --> 00:12:06,602
citation.

316
00:12:06,951 --> 00:12:08,883
The page is clear that the name

317
00:12:08,883 --> 00:12:11,251
broke the tradition of bull names that

318
00:12:11,251 --> 00:12:12,559
began with the Miura.

319
00:12:12,739 --> 00:12:14,703
So the car that arrived as he

320
00:12:14,703 --> 00:12:17,478
was leaving also departed from the naming

321
00:12:17,478 --> 00:12:19,171
habit of his own company.

322
00:12:19,351 --> 00:12:20,831
We should be careful:

323
00:12:20,831 --> 00:12:23,156
our sources do not say whether he

324
00:12:23,156 --> 00:12:24,425
approved the name.

325
00:12:24,605 --> 00:12:26,288
That detail is missing.

326
00:12:26,637 --> 00:12:30,295
After selling, Ferruccio Lamborghini retired.

327
00:12:30,475 --> 00:12:33,923
Wikipedia says that in nineteen seventy-four he

328
00:12:33,923 --> 00:12:36,711
moved to a roughly seven hundred forty

329
00:12:36,711 --> 00:12:38,912
acre estate called La Fiorita,

330
00:12:38,912 --> 00:12:41,994
on Lake Trasimeno in Castiglione del Lago,

331
00:12:41,994 --> 00:12:44,415
in Umbria, where he made wine and

332
00:12:44,415 --> 00:12:44,929
hunted.

333
00:12:45,109 --> 00:12:48,772
Many retellings place the winery in Tuscany,

334
00:12:48,772 --> 00:12:51,519
but the page we used says Umbria,

335
00:12:51,519 --> 00:12:52,934
so we use Umbria.

336
00:12:53,284 --> 00:12:55,434
He died of a heart attack at

337
00:12:55,434 --> 00:12:59,120
Silvestrini Hospital in Perugia on the twentieth

338
00:12:59,120 --> 00:13:01,807
of February, nineteen ninety-three,

339
00:13:01,807 --> 00:13:05,108
aged seventy-six, and is buried in Renazzo,

340
00:13:05,108 --> 00:13:06,183
the page says.

341
00:13:06,363 --> 00:13:07,593
By our arithmetic,

342
00:13:07,593 --> 00:13:10,531
he lived about nineteen years after leaving

343
00:13:10,531 --> 00:13:11,693
the car business,

344
00:13:11,693 --> 00:13:14,494
and the Countach stayed in production for

345
00:13:14,494 --> 00:13:15,588
sixteen of them,

346
00:13:15,588 --> 00:13:17,091
until nineteen ninety.

347
00:13:17,440 --> 00:13:20,122
His family carries on in other ways.

348
00:13:20,302 --> 00:13:22,480
Wikipedia says his son Tonino,

349
00:13:22,480 --> 00:13:24,585
born in nineteen forty-seven,

350
00:13:24,585 --> 00:13:27,053
runs a clothing line and the Museo

351
00:13:27,053 --> 00:13:29,885
Lamborghini, and his daughter Patrizia,

352
00:13:29,885 --> 00:13:31,990
born when he was fifty-eight,

353
00:13:31,990 --> 00:13:34,385
runs the family winery in Umbria.

354
00:13:34,565 --> 00:13:35,591
His first wife,

355
00:13:35,591 --> 00:13:38,600
Clelia Monti, died in childbirth in nineteen

356
00:13:38,600 --> 00:13:41,473
forty-seven, and he met Annita Borgatti in

357
00:13:41,473 --> 00:13:44,961
nineteen forty-eight, who helped run his businesses

358
00:13:44,961 --> 00:13:46,465
for over thirty years.

359
00:13:46,815 --> 00:13:50,376
The Countach outlived his involvement by sixteen

360
00:13:50,376 --> 00:13:50,821
years.

361
00:13:51,001 --> 00:13:54,072
Wikipedia says it was produced from nineteen

362
00:13:54,072 --> 00:13:56,305
seventy-four to nineteen ninety,

363
00:13:56,305 --> 00:13:57,421
in six variants,

364
00:13:57,421 --> 00:13:58,957
and that one thousand,

365
00:13:58,957 --> 00:14:01,888
nine hundred eighty-three cars were built,

366
00:14:01,888 --> 00:14:03,563
including the prototype.

367
00:14:03,743 --> 00:14:06,779
Substantially more than half were built in

368
00:14:06,779 --> 00:14:08,224
the last five years.

369
00:14:08,404 --> 00:14:10,417
The car that was launched as he

370
00:14:10,417 --> 00:14:12,755
left sold best after the company had

371
00:14:12,755 --> 00:14:13,664
changed hands.

372
00:14:14,013 --> 00:14:16,361
Wikipedia gives the breakdown:

373
00:14:16,361 --> 00:14:19,648
one hundred fifty-seven L P four hundreds,

374
00:14:19,648 --> 00:14:22,857
two hundred thirty-seven L P four hundred

375
00:14:22,857 --> 00:14:25,753
Ss, three hundred twenty-one L P five

376
00:14:25,753 --> 00:14:29,431
hundred Ss, six hundred ten Quattrovalvoles and

377
00:14:29,431 --> 00:14:33,658
six hundred fifty-seven twenty-fifth Anniversary cars.

378
00:14:33,838 --> 00:14:35,088
By our arithmetic,

379
00:14:35,088 --> 00:14:37,729
the final two variants account for one

380
00:14:37,729 --> 00:14:40,439
thousand, two hundred sixty-seven cars,

381
00:14:40,439 --> 00:14:43,079
about sixty-four percent of the total.

382
00:14:43,429 --> 00:14:44,892
The American market mattered.

383
00:14:45,072 --> 00:14:47,612
The page says the United States was

384
00:14:47,612 --> 00:14:49,715
Lamborghini's largest market,

385
00:14:49,715 --> 00:14:52,327
though the Countach was not built to

386
00:14:52,327 --> 00:14:53,343
U S standards,

387
00:14:53,343 --> 00:14:55,664
so owners paid to modify cars in

388
00:14:55,664 --> 00:14:57,115
the grey-market era,

389
00:14:57,115 --> 00:15:00,815
from nineteen seventy-six to nineteen eighty-eight.

390
00:15:00,995 --> 00:15:04,684
The first U S-specification factory car arrived

391
00:15:04,684 --> 00:15:06,568
in nineteen eighty-five.

392
00:15:06,748 --> 00:15:09,678
A Countach also paced the Monaco Grand

393
00:15:09,678 --> 00:15:13,610
Prix from nineteen eighty to nineteen eighty-three.

394
00:15:13,960 --> 00:15:16,676
Today, the early cars are valuable.

395
00:15:16,856 --> 00:15:19,510
RM Sotheby's sold Rod Stewart's L P

396
00:15:19,510 --> 00:15:23,528
four hundred Periscopio for eight hundred eighty-five

397
00:15:23,528 --> 00:15:26,409
thousand dollars in twenty twenty-six,

398
00:15:26,409 --> 00:15:29,517
and Hagerty shows one million dollars for

399
00:15:29,517 --> 00:15:32,019
a good nineteen seventy-five car.

400
00:15:32,199 --> 00:15:34,279
A Miura S V sold in Paris

401
00:15:34,279 --> 00:15:35,778
for three million,

402
00:15:35,778 --> 00:15:38,524
seven hundred seventeen thousand,

403
00:15:38,524 --> 00:15:40,105
five hundred euros.

404
00:15:40,285 --> 00:15:43,304
The company he sold in nineteen seventy-two

405
00:15:43,304 --> 00:15:45,901
was worth about one point two million

406
00:15:45,901 --> 00:15:47,797
dollars, by our arithmetic.

407
00:15:48,146 --> 00:15:50,547
What kind of car did his engineers

408
00:15:50,547 --> 00:15:51,606
actually build?

409
00:15:51,786 --> 00:15:55,022
Wikipedia says the Countach chassis was a

410
00:15:55,022 --> 00:15:58,653
full space frame of welded round-section steel

411
00:15:58,653 --> 00:15:59,205
tubing.

412
00:15:59,385 --> 00:16:02,410
The chassis for both the prototype and

413
00:16:02,410 --> 00:16:05,196
the production L P four hundred was

414
00:16:05,196 --> 00:16:06,628
built by Marchesi,

415
00:16:06,628 --> 00:16:09,335
and the prototype body by Bertone.

416
00:16:09,515 --> 00:16:13,242
The five-speed gearbox had Porsche-type synchromesh.

417
00:16:13,422 --> 00:16:16,548
So the parts came from several suppliers,

418
00:16:16,548 --> 00:16:18,530
assembled by a small team.

419
00:16:18,880 --> 00:16:21,075
The engine was turned around from the

420
00:16:21,075 --> 00:16:21,550
Miura's.

421
00:16:21,730 --> 00:16:25,021
In contrast to the Miura's transverse engine,

422
00:16:25,021 --> 00:16:26,045
the page says,

423
00:16:26,045 --> 00:16:28,385
the Countach's was longitudinal,

424
00:16:28,385 --> 00:16:31,529
with the transmission between the two seats

425
00:16:31,529 --> 00:16:34,235
and a driveshaft that ran through the

426
00:16:34,235 --> 00:16:37,380
engine's oil sump to the rear differential.

427
00:16:37,560 --> 00:16:40,521
The page lists better weight distribution,

428
00:16:40,521 --> 00:16:43,976
a shorter wheelbase and easier maintenance access

429
00:16:43,976 --> 00:16:46,302
as the advantages of that layout.

430
00:16:46,653 --> 00:16:48,150
The doors lifted up.

431
00:16:48,330 --> 00:16:51,759
Wikipedia says scissor doors first appeared on

432
00:16:51,759 --> 00:16:56,007
Marcello Gandini's nineteen sixty-eight Alfa Romeo Carabo

433
00:16:56,007 --> 00:16:58,989
concept, and that the Countach made them

434
00:16:58,989 --> 00:16:59,511
famous.

435
00:16:59,691 --> 00:17:03,649
Engineers studied a kick-out windscreen and explosive

436
00:17:03,649 --> 00:17:05,889
bolts for escaping a rollover,

437
00:17:05,889 --> 00:17:07,458
but neither was used.

438
00:17:07,638 --> 00:17:10,550
And because rear visibility was so poor,

439
00:17:10,550 --> 00:17:12,734
the prototype and the L P four

440
00:17:12,734 --> 00:17:15,937
hundred had a periscope supplied by Donnelly

441
00:17:15,937 --> 00:17:16,519
Mirrors.

442
00:17:16,870 --> 00:17:18,913
The prototype did not survive.

443
00:17:19,093 --> 00:17:21,744
The L P five hundred was crash-tested

444
00:17:21,744 --> 00:17:24,181
at the British test centre MIRA on

445
00:17:24,181 --> 00:17:26,044
the twenty-first of March,

446
00:17:26,044 --> 00:17:29,842
nineteen seventy-four, to win European type approval,

447
00:17:29,842 --> 00:17:31,132
and was destroyed.

448
00:17:31,312 --> 00:17:32,692
By our arithmetic,

449
00:17:32,692 --> 00:17:35,527
that was three years after its debut.

450
00:17:35,707 --> 00:17:36,852
At the same time,

451
00:17:36,852 --> 00:17:38,535
as far as the dates show,

452
00:17:38,535 --> 00:17:40,959
the man who had launched the project

453
00:17:40,959 --> 00:17:43,249
was selling his last shares in the

454
00:17:43,249 --> 00:17:43,787
company.

455
00:17:44,138 --> 00:17:46,808
The first production figures were modest.

456
00:17:46,988 --> 00:17:49,831
Wikipedia says one hundred fifty-seven L P

457
00:17:49,831 --> 00:17:51,523
four hundreds were built,

458
00:17:51,523 --> 00:17:53,959
and that fifty orders were placed at

459
00:17:53,959 --> 00:17:56,531
the nineteen seventy-four Geneva show.

460
00:17:56,711 --> 00:17:59,798
The RM Sotheby's catalogue for a Periscopio

461
00:17:59,798 --> 00:18:03,101
says about one hundred fifty first-series cars

462
00:18:03,101 --> 00:18:06,475
were built, according to the Countach Register.

463
00:18:06,655 --> 00:18:07,906
By our arithmetic,

464
00:18:07,906 --> 00:18:10,339
fifty orders at Geneva were about a

465
00:18:10,339 --> 00:18:12,147
third of the first series.

466
00:18:12,497 --> 00:18:15,620
After Ferruccio, the car carried on under

467
00:18:15,620 --> 00:18:16,154
others.

468
00:18:16,334 --> 00:18:19,028
Wikipedia lists a Swiss distributor,

469
00:18:19,028 --> 00:18:23,144
Max Bobnar, who commissioned two turbocharged Countachs

470
00:18:23,144 --> 00:18:26,736
between nineteen eighty and nineteen eighty-two.

471
00:18:26,916 --> 00:18:29,287
One was shown at Geneva in nineteen

472
00:18:29,287 --> 00:18:32,741
eighty-four with a claimed seven hundred fifty-nine

473
00:18:32,741 --> 00:18:35,382
metric horsepower in the text and seven

474
00:18:35,382 --> 00:18:37,481
hundred forty-six in the table.

475
00:18:37,661 --> 00:18:39,974
Those were not factory products,

476
00:18:39,974 --> 00:18:42,359
but they show the car's appeal to

477
00:18:42,359 --> 00:18:43,443
outside buyers.

478
00:18:43,793 --> 00:18:47,197
In nineteen eighty-five the factory built the

479
00:18:47,197 --> 00:18:49,390
five thousand Quattrovalvole,

480
00:18:49,390 --> 00:18:50,827
with five thousand,

481
00:18:50,827 --> 00:18:54,610
one hundred sixty-seven cubic centimetres and four

482
00:18:54,610 --> 00:18:57,409
hundred fifty-five metric horsepower,

483
00:18:57,409 --> 00:19:00,359
and later the twenty-fifth Anniversary,

484
00:19:00,359 --> 00:19:03,763
which marked twenty-five years of the marque,

485
00:19:03,763 --> 00:19:05,805
restyled by Horacio Pagani.

486
00:19:05,985 --> 00:19:08,276
The page says it was possibly the

487
00:19:08,276 --> 00:19:09,387
fastest variant.

488
00:19:09,567 --> 00:19:11,494
None of these carried his hand.

489
00:19:11,674 --> 00:19:13,423
They were the work of the men

490
00:19:13,423 --> 00:19:15,412
and companies who came after him.

491
00:19:15,763 --> 00:19:18,340
A few ideas never reached the road.

492
00:19:18,520 --> 00:19:21,030
Wikipedia lists the L one fifty,

493
00:19:21,030 --> 00:19:24,718
an update from nineteen eighty-four to nineteen

494
00:19:24,718 --> 00:19:28,640
eighty-seven that Patrick Mimran rejected for cost

495
00:19:28,640 --> 00:19:30,445
reasons, and the Q V X,

496
00:19:30,445 --> 00:19:32,720
a Group C racing car that was

497
00:19:32,720 --> 00:19:34,681
not built by the factory.

498
00:19:34,861 --> 00:19:38,399
In twenty twenty-one the Countach name returned

499
00:19:38,399 --> 00:19:40,356
on the L P I eight hundred

500
00:19:40,356 --> 00:19:42,840
dash four, a six point five litre

501
00:19:42,840 --> 00:19:45,926
V twelve with a forty-eight volt electric

502
00:19:45,926 --> 00:19:46,378
motor.

503
00:19:46,729 --> 00:19:48,762
Now we should be honest about what

504
00:19:48,762 --> 00:19:49,898
we cannot tell you.

505
00:19:50,078 --> 00:19:52,344
The sources we found do not say

506
00:19:52,344 --> 00:19:56,071
what Ferruccio Lamborghini thought of the Countach,

507
00:19:56,071 --> 00:19:58,337
whether he saw it as a success,

508
00:19:58,337 --> 00:20:00,822
or why exactly he sold in nineteen

509
00:20:00,822 --> 00:20:04,184
seventy-four beyond calling him disillusioned.

510
00:20:04,364 --> 00:20:05,883
They do not give the price of

511
00:20:05,883 --> 00:20:06,721
the second sale.

512
00:20:06,901 --> 00:20:08,889
They do not say how the Rossetti

513
00:20:08,889 --> 00:20:10,256
and Leimer years went.

514
00:20:10,607 --> 00:20:13,172
They also differ on small things.

515
00:20:13,352 --> 00:20:15,597
The date of the tractor sale to

516
00:20:15,597 --> 00:20:18,565
SAME, nineteen seventy-two on the page we

517
00:20:18,565 --> 00:20:22,257
used and nineteen seventy-three in many retellings.

518
00:20:22,437 --> 00:20:24,475
The location of his winery,

519
00:20:24,475 --> 00:20:27,569
Umbria on the page and Tuscany elsewhere.

520
00:20:27,749 --> 00:20:29,641
The nature of his studies.

521
00:20:29,821 --> 00:20:32,387
And the origin of the Countach name.

522
00:20:32,567 --> 00:20:35,068
If a video tells you these things

523
00:20:35,068 --> 00:20:36,736
with total confidence,

524
00:20:36,736 --> 00:20:39,161
be sceptical, including of ours.

525
00:20:39,512 --> 00:20:41,218
One more point of caution.

526
00:20:41,398 --> 00:20:44,463
The Ferrari clutch story is recalled from

527
00:20:44,463 --> 00:20:47,603
an interview thirty years after the event,

528
00:20:47,603 --> 00:20:48,724
the page notes.

529
00:20:48,904 --> 00:20:50,165
By our arithmetic,

530
00:20:50,165 --> 00:20:53,806
nineteen ninety-one minus the early nineteen sixties

531
00:20:53,806 --> 00:20:55,487
is roughly thirty years.

532
00:20:55,667 --> 00:20:58,612
Memory changes, and a good story improves

533
00:20:58,612 --> 00:20:59,905
with each telling.

534
00:21:00,085 --> 00:21:01,722
That does not make it false.

535
00:21:01,902 --> 00:21:03,416
It means it is a memory,

536
00:21:03,416 --> 00:21:04,363
not a document.

537
00:21:04,713 --> 00:21:06,997
And the larger legend has a similar

538
00:21:06,997 --> 00:21:07,650
structure.

539
00:21:07,830 --> 00:21:09,394
A man is dismissed,

540
00:21:09,394 --> 00:21:11,534
so he builds a better car,

541
00:21:11,534 --> 00:21:14,250
and then the car becomes an icon.

542
00:21:14,430 --> 00:21:15,324
It is neat.

543
00:21:15,504 --> 00:21:18,498
The sources suggest a messier reality:

544
00:21:18,498 --> 00:21:20,703
an industrialist with money,

545
00:21:20,703 --> 00:21:23,145
a team of engineers who built a

546
00:21:23,145 --> 00:21:24,642
prototype at night,

547
00:21:24,642 --> 00:21:27,714
a tractor firm hit by cancelled orders,

548
00:21:27,714 --> 00:21:30,235
and a car company he sold within

549
00:21:30,235 --> 00:21:31,259
eleven years.

550
00:21:31,439 --> 00:21:33,262
Both stories can be told.

551
00:21:33,442 --> 00:21:36,042
Only one fits all the documents.

552
00:21:36,393 --> 00:21:38,470
So how did a tractor maker end

553
00:21:38,470 --> 00:21:40,201
